# Reverse Cymatic Decoder — Physics Specification

Scope: pattern → candidate vibration modes → frequencies on a reference resonator.
All formulas below are implementable as written. SI units throughout.

---

## 1. Forward models

### 1a. Circular membrane (ideal drum)

Governing equation: wave equation on a 2-D disk, fixed rim u(R, θ) = 0.

Mode shape (standing wave, separation of variables):

    u_{n,m}(r, θ, t) = A · J_n(z_{n,m} · r / R) · cos(nθ + φ) · cos(ω_{n,m} t + ψ)

- n = 0,1,2,...  azimuthal order → n nodal **diameters** (2n nodal radial spokes at angles where cos(nθ+φ)=0)
- m = 1,2,3,...  radial order → m−1 nodal **circles** (interior zeros of J_n), plus the fixed rim
- φ is an arbitrary rotation (degeneracy of cos/sin pairs for n ≥ 1)
- Eigencondition: J_n(z) = 0 at r = R  ⟹  kR = z_{n,m}
- Frequency:

      f_{n,m} = z_{n,m} · c / (2πR),   c = √(T/σ)

  where T = tension per unit length [N/m], σ = areal mass density [kg/m²].

**Bessel zeros z_{n,m}** (numerical, 6 dp; n = azimuthal, m = radial):

| n \ m | m=1 | m=2 | m=3 | m=4 |
|-------|-----------|-----------|-----------|-----------|
| 0 | 2.404826 | 5.520078 | 8.653728 | 11.791534 |
| 1 | 3.831706 | 7.015587 | 10.173468 | 13.323692 |
| 2 | 5.135622 | 8.417244 | 11.619841 | 14.795952 |
| 3 | 6.380162 | 9.761023 | 13.015201 | 16.223466 |
| 4 | 7.588342 | 11.064709 | 14.372537 | 17.615966 |
| 5 | 8.771484 | 12.338604 | 15.700174 | 18.980134 |

Asymptotic (useful for z beyond the table, valid for large m at fixed n):

    z_{n,m} ≈ β − (μ−1)/(8β) − 4(μ−1)(7μ−31)/(3(8β)³),
    β = π(m + n/2 − 1/4),  μ = 4n²

Properties to encode as constants:
- Frequency ratios within one resonator are **pure numbers**: f_{n,m}/f_{0,1} = z_{n,m}/z_{0,1}. E.g. f_{1,1}/f_{0,1} = 3.831706/2.404826 = 1.59334 (this is why drums are inharmonic — ratios are irrational, unlike strings).
- Interlacing: z_{n,m} < z_{n+1,m} < z_{n,m+1} (zeros interleave across n).
- Nodal-line counting rule (for reverse model): a pure (n,m) mode shows exactly n nodal diameters and m−1 concentric nodal circles.

### 1b. Square plate / Chladni approximation

Two distinct dispersion laws — **do not conflate them**:

**(i) Membrane / simply-supported model (default recommendation):**

    u_{p,q}(x,y) = sin(pπx/L)·sin(qπy/L)
    f_{p,q} = (c/2L)·√(p²+q²)

Nodal lines of a single mode are a rectangular grid: (p−1) vertical lines x = kL/p and (q−1) horizontal lines y = kL/q. The curved, diagonally-symmetric Chladni figures arise only from **degenerate superpositions** (see 1c).

**(ii) Thin free plate (true Chladni physics):** biharmonic equation ∇⁴u = k⁴u with free-edge boundary conditions. No closed-form eigenfunctions on a square; the dispersion is

    f_{p,q} ≈ C·(h/L²)·√(E/(12ρ(1−ν²)))·(p²+q²)

i.e. **f ∝ (p²+q²)** (quadratic in mode numbers) instead of √(p²+q²). Free-edge nodal lines are not sin·sin grids; the sin·sin form is only a usable *shape ansatz* for interior structure far from edges.

**Recommendation:** default to model (i) — closed form, invertible, and the superposition model reproduces the visual phenomenology of Chladni figures well. Expose model (ii) as an option that rescales only the frequency mapping (replace √(p²+q²) by (p²+q²), with its own constant C·h/L²), while keeping the same pattern-matching machinery. Flag to the user that (ii)'s shapes are approximate.

### 1c. Amplitude rendering and nodal-line extraction

Sand collects at nodes (|u| ≈ 0, time-averaged). Render the time-independent spatial amplitude:

    A(x,y) = Σ_k w_k · u_k(x,y)          (real superposition; ± sign and phase absorbed in w_k)
    nodal set = { (x,y) : |A(x,y)| ≤ ε · max|A| }

Implementation parameters:
- **ε (threshold):** default 0.05–0.15 of max amplitude. Physically ε encodes sand size + drive amplitude: grains hop until acceleration < threshold, so the *visible* nodal line is a band of finite width. Make ε user-tunable; report that all Chladni figures are threshold-dependent.
- **Damping weights:** in steady state under harmonic drive at frequency ω_d, mode k is excited with amplitude

      w_k ∝ F_k / √((ω_k² − ω_d²)² + (2ζ_k ω_k ω_d)²)

  where F_k = modal overlap of the drive, ζ_k = modal damping ratio (typ. 0.005–0.05 for plates). For a decoder, the practical simplification: **w_k are free fit parameters**, optionally regularized by a prior w_k ∝ ω_k^{−α}, α ≈ 1–2 (high modes are harder to excite).
- **Degenerate pairs:** for n ≥ 1 on the circle, cos(nθ) and sin(nθ) are degenerate — always superpose them (relative weight = rotation φ, which is free). On the square, (p,q) and (q,p) are exactly degenerate; a superposition w₁·sin(pπx/L)sin(qπy/L) ± w₂·sin(qπx/L)sin(pπy/L) with w₁ = ±w₂ produces the classic diagonal Chladni figures. **Always include both members of a degenerate pair in any candidate.**

